{"key":"st","num":"44","title":"Sao Tome and Principe","blurb":"Every place GeoNames records above 500 people in Sao Tome and Principe: 51 towns and villages, grouped by region and ordered by population.","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_and_Pr%C3%ADncipe","d":"São Tomé and Príncipe, officially the Democratic Republic of Sao Tome and Principe, is an island country in the Gulf of Guinea, off the western equatorial coast of Central Africa. It consists of two archipelagos around the two main islands of São Tomé and Príncipe, about 81 nautical miles (150 km; 93 mi) apart and about 135 and 121 nautical miles (250 and 224 km; 155 and 139 mi) off the northwestern coast of Gabon. With a population of 201,800 (2018 official estimate), São Tomé and Príncipe is the second-smallest and second-least populous African sovereign state after Seychelles.\nThe islands were uninhabited until Portuguese explorers João de Santarém and Pedro Escobar discovered them on 21 December 1470. Gradually colonized and settled throughout the 16th century, they collectively served as a vital commercial and trade centre for the Atlantic slave trade. The rich volcanic soil and proximity to the equator made São Tomé and Príncipe ideal for sugar cultivation, followed later by other cash crops such as coffee and cocoa. The lucrative plantation economy was heavily dependent upon enslaved Africans. Cycles of social unrest and economic instability throughout the 19th and 20th centuries culminated in peaceful independence in 1975 as a one-party communist state, which would remain in place until 1990. São Tomé and Príncipe has since remained one of Africa's most stable and democratic countries with a developing economy and a medium Human Development Index.\nThe people of São Tomé and Príncipe are predominantly of African and mestiço (mixed European and African) descent, with most practicing Christianity. The legacy of Portuguese rule is also visible in the country's culture, customs, and music, which fuse both European and African influences. São Tomé and Príncipe is a founding member state of the Community of Portuguese Language Countries.","groups":[{"name":"São Tomé Island","items":[{"n":"São Tomé","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2410763","p":53300,"t":"capital","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9"},{"n":"Neves","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2410972","p":8391,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neves,_S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_and_Pr%C3%ADncipe"},{"n":"Água Izé","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2411305","p":7445,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%81gua_Iz%C3%A9"},{"n":"Folha Fede","u":"https://www.geonames.org/11204006","p":6685,"t":"village"},{"n":"Riboque","u":"https://www.geonames.org/11204197","p":4640,"t":"village"},{"n":"Almas","u":"https://www.geonames.org/12718718","p":4601,"t":"village"},{"n":"Pantufo","u":"https://www.geonames.org/12718734","p":3977,"t":"village"},{"n":"Dona Eugénia","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2411155","p":3605,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dona_Eugenia"},{"n":"São Marçal","u":"https://www.geonames.org/11204105","p":2866,"t":"village"},{"n":"Rosema","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2410849","p":2587,"t":"village"},{"n":"Agua Porca","u":"https://www.geonames.org/11204142","p":2048,"t":"village"},{"n":"Almeirim","u":"https://www.geonames.org/11204020","p":1591,"t":"village"},{"n":"Piedade","u":"https://www.geonames.org/13151892","p":1408,"t":"village"},{"n":"Micolo","u":"https://www.geonames.org/11204288","p":1404,"t":"village"},{"n":"Ribeira Afonso","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2410860","p":1300,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribeira_Afonso"},{"n":"Changra","u":"https://www.geonames.org/12718722","p":1224,"t":"village"},{"n":"Lemos","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2411049","p":1136,"t":"village","w":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemos,_S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_and_Pr%C3%ADncipe"},{"n":"Santo Amaro","u":"https://www.geonames.org/2410809","p":1063,"t":"vil